/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A Gunn diode is a slab of n-type GaAs (or InP) of length L with ohmic contacts at both ends.
   - At low field all electrons sit in the central valley and the drift velocity rises with field.
   - A uniform field in the NDR region is unstable.
   - f in hertz, vd the domain drift velocity (about 10⁵ m/s) and L the active length in metres.
   - The diode sits in a cavity that fixes the frequency; a varactor or YIG sphere tunes it.
   - An IMPATT (impact-ionisation avalanche transit-time) diode is a reverse-biased junction held at breakdown, for example the Read structure p⁺–n–i–n⁺: a thin avalanche region followed by a drift region of length Ld.
   - vs ≈ 10⁵ m/s in silicon and Ld the drift length in metres; a 5 µm drift region gives about 10 GHz.
   - IMPATTs give the most power of any solid-state source at millimetre waves, with efficiency typically several per cent up to about 15 %, but the avalanche makes them noisy.
   - A tunnel diode is a p–n junction doped so heavily that its depletion layer is only about 10 nm thick, so electrons tunnel through the barrier.
   - Bias the diode at a DC voltage between Vp and Vv, inside the negative-slope region, and connect a resonator: it oscillates.
*/
