/* book Worked example: Q: A GaAs Gunn diode must oscillate in the transit-time mode at 10 GHz. Take vd = 10⁷ cm/s and a threshold field of 3.2 kV/cm. Find (a) the active length, (b) the threshold voltage, (c) the field at a bias of 8 V, (d) the least doping for the transit-time mode, and (e) the efficiency if the diode draws 0.5 A at 8 V and gives 100 mW of RF.
   1. L = vd/f = 10⁵ m/s ÷ 10¹⁰ Hz = 10⁻⁵ m = 10 µm.
   2. Threshold voltage = Eth × L = 3.2 × 10⁵ V/m × 10⁻⁵ m = 3.2 V.
   3. At 8 V the field is 8 V/10⁻³ cm = 8 kV/cm, inside the NDR region (above 3.2 kV/cm).
   4. Transit-time mode needs n₀L > 10¹² cm⁻². With L = 10⁻³ cm: n₀ > 10¹² ÷ 10⁻³ = 10¹⁵ cm⁻³.
   5. DC input = 8 × 0.5 = 4 W; η = 0.1/4 = 0.025 = 2.5 %. The other 3.9 W heats the diode, so it needs a heat sink.
   Answer: L = 10 µm, threshold 3.2 V, 8 kV/cm at 8 V bias, n₀ above 10¹⁵ cm⁻³, efficiency 2.5 %. */
