/* book exam traps: Calling the Gunn diode a p–n junction device. It is a bulk n-type slab; its negative resistance comes from electrons transferring to the slow satellite valley. | Giving IMPATT the Gunn mechanism, or the reverse. IMPATT = avalanche plus transit delay in a reverse-biased junction; Gunn = field-driven valley transfer. | Saying TRAPATT runs at low frequency "because of harmonics". It runs below the IMPATT range because extracting the trapped plasma takes much longer than a transit time. | Biasing a tunnel diode by current. The DC voltage must lie between the peak voltage Vp and the valley voltage Vv. | Quoting tube-like efficiencies for diodes. Gunn sources give a few per cent; 25 % would be unrealistic. */
