/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - In a hybrid MIC the substrate carries the passive pattern: microstrip or coplanar lines, couplers, filters, matching stubs and thin-film resistors.
   - Line geometry sets impedance: a microstrip of width w on height h and permittivity εr has an impedance Z₀ and an effective permittivity εeff between 1 and εr, because part of the field is in air; the guided wavelength is λg = λ₀/√εeff.
   - A monolithic microwave integrated circuit forms transistors, resistors, capacitors, inductors and lines in or on one semi-insulating wafer, usually GaAs (resistivity above 10⁷ Ω·cm, εr = 12.9), also GaN on SiC for power, InP above 100 GHz, 
   - A typical GaAs process: form the active layer; isolate devices; make ohmic contacts and gates; deposit nitride and build MIM capacitors and resistors; pattern the first metal (lines, spiral turns, capacitor plates); plate the airbridge meta
   - Distributed lines are long on a chip.
   - Packaging is part of the design.
   - Test structures on the same wafer settle the process: a microstrip ring or half-wave resonator gives εeff from its resonant frequency and the loss from its Q.
*/
