/* book Worked example: Q: A GaAs MMIC matching network needs (a) a 2 pF MIM capacitor using silicon nitride with εr = 7.5 and d = 0.2 µm, and (b) a 100 Ω thin-film resistor in a 50 Ω/□ film, 10 µm wide. Find the capacitor area and side, and the resistor length. (c) Compare with a quarter-wave line at 10 GHz on the same chip (εeff = 8.4).
   1. Capacitance per area: ε₀εr/d = 8.854 × 10⁻¹² × 7.5/0.2 × 10⁻⁶ = 3.32 × 10⁻⁴ F/m² = 0.332 fF/µm².
   2. Area A = C/(ε₀εr/d) = 2 × 10⁻¹²/3.32 × 10⁻⁴ = 6.024 × 10⁻⁹ m² = 6024 µm², a square about 77.6 µm on a side.
   3. Resistor: R = Rs × (l/w), so l/w = 100/50 = 2 squares; l = 2 × 10 µm = 20 µm.
   4. Quarter-wave line: λg = λ₀/√εeff = 30 mm/√8.4 = 10.35 mm; λg/4 = 2.59 mm.
   5. The capacitor and resistor fit in less than 0.1 mm; the line is 2.6 mm long, which on a 2 mm chip would have to meander. That is why low-GHz MMICs match with lumped parts.
   Answer: MIM capacitor 6024 µm² (about 78 µm square); resistor 20 µm long by 10 µm wide; a 10 GHz quarter-wave line is 2.59 mm long. */
