/* book exam traps: Calling every flat microwave board an MMIC. It is monolithic only if the transistors are made in the wafer; a board with mounted transistors is a hybrid MIC. | Treating on-chip spirals as ideal. Their Q is only about 10 to 20, so matching networks built from them lose signal and add noise. | Using εr where εeff belongs. Microstrip λg = λ₀/√εeff, with εeff < εr because part of the field is in air. | Forgetting that bond wires and vias are inductors, about 1 nH per millimetre: 0.5 nH is already 31 Ω at 10 GHz. | Mixing up the airbridge and the first metal. The airbridge is the plated second metal that crosses over lines with air beneath; lines, spiral turns and lower capacitor plates are the first metal. */
